<title>USA: Mexico halts imports of sorghum via Texas-USDA.</title>
<headline>Mexico halts imports of sorghum via Texas-USDA.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1997-05-09</dateline>
<text>
<p>Mexico has halted imports of U.S. sorghum from Texas, a high-ranking U.S. Agriculture Department official said Friday.</p>
<p>The cut-off appeared to be tied to Mexico's fight against an outbreak of the fungus ergot. The fungus reportedly affected a small amount of land planted to sorghum but was found in several regions.  </p>
<p>Some 42,800 tonnes of U.S. sorghum were shipped to Mexico during 1996/97 through May 1, according  to USDA export sales data.</p>
<p>U.S. sorghum shipments ran into an unofficial snag last fall when Mexican authorities allegedly slowed down processing of permits for the imports.</p>
<p>Ergot was detected in Mexican sorghum early this year.</p>
<p>(Charles Abbott, Washington commodities, 202-898-8319))</p>
